/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - For an ideal gas at fixed temperature, the chemical potential rises logarithmically with pressure: μ = μ°(T) + RT ln(P/P°).
   - A real gas breaks that assumption.
   - μᵢ is the chemical potential of species i in J/mol; μᵢ° is its value in the reference state at the same T; R = 8.314 J/(mol·K); fᵢ is the fugacity in bar or Pa; fᵢ° is the fugacity of the reference state.
   - The definition is completed by a limit: fᵢ → Pᵢ (the partial pressure) as P → 0, where every gas becomes ideal.
   - φᵢ is the fugacity coefficient, dimensionless; Pᵢ is the partial pressure, yᵢP for a gas mixture or P for a pure gas.
   - φ → 1 as P → 0, and an ideal gas has φ = 1 at every pressure, so any value away from 1 measures non-ideality.
   - At fixed temperature dG = V dP for any fluid and dGⁱᵍ = (RT/P) dP for an ideal gas.
   - V is molar volume in m³/mol; R = 8.314 J/(mol·K); T in K; P in Pa; Z = PV/RT is the compressibility factor at the same T and P.
   - Read the sign.
   - Two routes give the integral quickly.
*/
