/* book Worked example 2: Q: Estimate the fugacity coefficient and fugacity of n-butane at 500 K and 15 bar from the generalized virial (Pitzer) correlation. For n-butane Tc = 425.1 K, Pc = 37.96 bar and ω = 0.200.
   1. Tᵣ = 500/425.1 = 1.1762; Pᵣ = 15/37.96 = 0.3952. Low Pᵣ and Tᵣ above 1: the virial correlation applies.
   2. B⁰ = 0.083 − 0.422/Tᵣ1.6 = 0.083 − 0.422/1.2967 = 0.083 − 0.3254 = −0.2425.
   3. B¹ = 0.139 − 0.172/Tᵣ4.2 = 0.139 − 0.172/1.9772 = 0.139 − 0.0870 = 0.0520.
   4. B⁰ + ωB¹ = −0.2425 + 0.200 × 0.0520 = −0.2321.
   5. ln φ = (Pᵣ/Tᵣ)(B⁰ + ωB¹) = (0.3952/1.1762)(−0.2321) = 0.3360 × (−0.2321) = −0.0780.
   6. φ = e−0.0780 = 0.925; f = φP = 0.925 × 15 bar = 13.9 bar.
   7. Check with B itself: B = (B⁰ + ωB¹)RTc/Pc = −2.16 × 10⁻⁴ m³/mol, and BP/RT = −2.16 × 10⁻⁴ × 15 × 10⁵/(8.314 × 500) = −0.0780, the same ln φ.
   Answer: φ = 0.925 and f = 13.9 bar: n-butane at 500 K and 15 bar escapes as if its pressure were 1.1 bar lower than it is. */
